
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Subject: <type>(<scope>): <imperative, lower case, no trailing period>
#
# Body: the WHY, not the what. `git diff` already shows the what.
# Keep mechanical changes (formatting, line endings, renames) in their own
# commits, never mixed with semantic ones.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THIS TEMPLATE ENFORCES NOTHING. It is a convenience that pre-fills the block
# below, and it is inert three ways over: git applies it only to commits
# written through the editor and never to `git commit -m` or `-F`; it does
# nothing at all until someone runs `git config commit.template .gitmessage`,
# which is per-machine config rather than tracked state; and no check anywhere
# reads the result. Nothing fails when the block is skipped.
#
# The obligation itself lives in CLAUDE.md's "Git workflow" section, attached
# to the Phase 1 report and the Phase 2 authorisation. Read it there.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Findings block is the LAST section of the body, before any trailer.
#
# `Findings: none` is MANDATORY -- never an absent section. An absent section
# cannot be told apart from a forgotten one.
#
# Two shapes. Keep one, delete the other:
#
#   Findings: none
#
#   Findings:
#   - <ID> -- <one line>. <MEASURED | REASONED | UNMEASURED>.
#
# A finding found by a scratchpad probe has no commit of its own; it lands in
# the docs commit that records the measurement. See CLAUDE.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Findings: none
